{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And behold, it was very good</b> — this refers to its permanence, as I have explained (above, verses 4, 10, 12). And the meaning of \"very\" is the sense of a plurality. And He added on this day to say so in summary, because in speaking in general terms some evil might be found in part of it, and He said that it was \"very good\" — its \"very\" is good. And this is their statement in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 9:5): \"'And behold, it was very good' — and behold, it was good: this is the angel of death.\" And similarly they mentioned there (ibid. 11): \"this is the evil inclination,\" and this is the attribute of punishment. And to this Onkelos also intended (Onkelos on Genesis 1:31), for he said here \"והא תקין לחדא\" — meaning that the order is thoroughly perfected, for evil is necessary for the permanence of good, in accordance with the concept stated (Ecclesiastes 3:1): \"To everything there is a season, and a time for every purpose under the heaven.\" And there are those who explain that because of the excellence of man, He added praise of his formation, for he is very good."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The reference to Genesis Rabbah 9:11 is given as (שם י\"א), i.e., 'there, section 11,' referring to a passage later in the same chapter of Genesis Rabbah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:15:31.615006541Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}